{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000173","statement":"Adult-onset Still's disease is an inflammatory condition characterized by high fevers, rash, sore throat, and joint pain.","source_span":"an inflammatory condition characterized by high fevers, rash, sore throat, and joint pain"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000173","statement":"Still's disease was named after English doctor George Still, who described the condition in children in 1896.","source_span":"named after an English doctor named George Still, who described the condition in children in 1896"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000173","statement":"Still's disease occurring in children under age 16 is now known as systemic onset juvenile rheumatoid arthritis (JRA).","source_span":"Still's disease which occurs in children (those under the age of 16) is now known as systemic onset juvenile rheumatoid arthritis (JRA)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000173","statement":"In 1971, the term \"adult Still's disease\" was used to describe adults with a condition similar to systemic onset JRA.","source_span":"In 1971, the term \"adult Still's disease\" was used to describe adults who had a condition similar to systemic onset JRA"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000173","statement":"The cause of adult-onset Still's disease is unknown and no risk factors for the disease have been identified.","source_span":"The cause of adult-onset Still's disease is unknown. No risk factors for the disease have been identified"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000173","statement":"There is no cure for adult-onset Still's disease, though treatment may offer symptom relief and help prevent complications.","source_span":"There's no cure for adult-onset Still's disease; however, treatment may offer symptom relief and help prevent complications"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000173","statement":"Some hypothesize adult-onset Still's disease results from or is triggered by a virus or other infectious agent, while others believe it is a hypersensitive or autoimmune disorder, with no conclusive evidence for either.","source_span":"Some hypothesize that the condition results from or is triggered by a virus or other infectious agent. Others believe that it is a hypersensitive or autoimmune disorder. To date, no conclusive evidence has been found"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000173","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Who first described Still's disease and in what year?","answer":"George Still, an English doctor, described the condition in children in 1896."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000173","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is systemic onset juvenile rheumatoid arthritis (JRA)?","answer":"It is the name now used for Still's disease when it occurs in children under the age of 16."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000173","fact_refs":[3],"question":"When was the term \"adult Still's disease\" first used?","answer":"In 1971, to describe adults with a condition similar to systemic onset JRA."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000173","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What are the main symptoms of adult-onset Still's disease?","answer":"High fevers, rash, sore throat, and joint pain."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000173","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Is there a cure for adult-onset Still's disease?","answer":"No, there is no cure, but treatment may offer symptom relief and help prevent complications."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000173","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are the two leading theories about what causes adult-onset Still's disease?","answer":"That it results from or is triggered by a virus or other infectious agent, or that it is a hypersensitive or autoimmune disorder; no conclusive evidence supports either theory."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000174","statement":"Adult-onset vitelliform macular dystrophy (AVMD) is an eye disorder affecting the macula that can cause progressive vision loss.","source_span":"AVMD affects an area of the retina called the macula, which is responsible for sharp central vision"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000174","statement":"Signs and symptoms of AVMD usually begin between ages 30 and 50 and include blurred and/or distorted vision that can progress to central vision loss.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms usually begin between ages 30 and 50 and include blurred and/or distorted vision, which can progress to central vision loss over time"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000174","statement":"AVMD has historically been characterized as caused by mutations in the PRPH2, BEST1, IMPG1, and IMPG2 genes.","source_span":"AVMD has been characterized as a genetic disorder caused by mutations in the PRPH2, BEST1, IMPG1, and IMPG2 genes"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000174","statement":"AVMD is additionally suspected to be associated with a single-nucleotide polymorphism in the HTRA1 gene, which is also associated with age-related macular degeneration.","source_span":"it is additionally suspected that AVMD might be associated with a single-nucleotide polymorphism (variant DNA sequence) in the HTRA1 gene. Single-nucleotide polymorphisms in the HTRA1 gene are additionally associated with age-related macular degeneration"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000174","statement":"When AVMD is caused by a known mutation inherited in an autosomal dominant manner, a person has a 50% chance with each pregnancy of passing along the altered gene to their child.","source_span":"a person with adult-onset macular dystrophy (AVMD) has a 50% chance with each pregnancy of passing along the altered gene to his or her child"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000174","statement":"Management of AVMD includes a comprehensive eye examination, including dilation, once or twice a year.","source_span":"Management for this condition should include a comprehensive eye examination, including dilation, once or twice a year"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000174","statement":"Intravitreal injections of either Ranibizumab or Bevacizumab may be effective in the short-term for AVMD, and transcorneal electrical stimulation has been found to improve visual acuity.","source_span":"Intravitreal injections of either Ranibizumab or Bevacizumab may be effective in the short-term. Transcorneal electrical stimulation has also been found to improve visual acuity"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000174","fact_refs":[1],"question":"At what age do signs and symptoms of adult-onset vitelliform macular dystrophy typically begin?","answer":"Usually between ages 30 and 50, including blurred and/or distorted vision that can progress to central vision loss."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000174","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which genes have historically been linked to causing AVMD?","answer":"PRPH2, BEST1, IMPG1, and IMPG2."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000174","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What other gene's variant is suspected to be associated with AVMD, and what other condition is it linked to?","answer":"A single-nucleotide polymorphism in the HTRA1 gene is suspected to be associated with AVMD; HTRA1 polymorphisms are also linked to age-related macular degeneration."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000174","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What is the chance of passing on AVMD to a child when it is caused by a known autosomal dominant mutation?","answer":"There is a 50% chance with each pregnancy of passing along the altered gene to the child."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000174","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What treatments may help improve visual acuity in people with AVMD?","answer":"Intravitreal injections of Ranibizumab or Bevacizumab may help short-term, and transcorneal electrical stimulation has been found to improve visual acuity."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000174","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How often should AVMD patients receive eye examinations as part of management?","answer":"Once or twice a year, including a comprehensive dilated eye examination."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000178","statement":"Afibrinogenemia is an inherited blood disorder in which the blood does not clot normally due to a deficiency of fibrinogen (factor I).","source_span":"an inherited blood disorder in which the blood does not clot normally. It occurs when there is a lack (deficiency) of a protein called fibrinogen (or factor I)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000178","statement":"Afibrinogenemia is thought to be transmitted as an autosomal recessive trait.","source_span":"Afibrinogenemia is thought to be transmitted as an autosomal recessive trait"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000178","statement":"In afibrinogenemia, fibrinogen levels are less than 0.1 g/L, with bleeding manifestations ranging from mild to severe.","source_span":"with fibrinogen levels less than 0.1 g/L, bleeding manifestations range from mild to severe"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000178","statement":"Umbilical cord hemorrhage frequently provides an early alert to afibrinogenemia.","source_span":"Umbilical cord hemorrhage frequently provides an early alert to the abnormality"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000178","statement":"Afibrinogenemia can result from mutations in one of the fibrinogen genes alpha (FGA), beta (FGB), or gamma (FGG).","source_span":"This defect in fibrinogen synthesis can result from mutations in one or another of the fibrinogen genes alpha (FGA), beta (FGB) or gamma (FGG)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000178","statement":"Afibrinogenemia is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, requiring inheritance of two abnormal genes, one from each parent.","source_span":"Afibrinogenemia is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, meaning that in order to be affected, an individual must have inherited two abnormal genes, one from each parent"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000178","statement":"Treatment for bleeding episodes in afibrinogenemia may include plasma or a blood product containing concentrated fibrinogen (cryoprecipitate) given via transfusion.","source_span":"patients may receive: The liquid portion of the blood (plasma) A blood product containing concentrated fibrinogen (cryoprecipitate) through a vein (transfusion)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000178","statement":"Individuals with afibrinogenemia should be vaccinated with the hepatitis B vaccine because transfusion increases the risk of hepatitis.","source_span":"Vaccination with the hepatitis B vaccine because transfusion increases the risk of hepatitis"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000178","fact_refs":[0,4],"question":"What causes afibrinogenemia?","answer":"A severe deficiency of fibrinogen (factor I), which can result from mutations in the fibrinogen genes FGA, FGB, or FGG."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000178","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How is afibrinogenemia inherited?","answer":"In an autosomal recessive manner, meaning an affected individual must inherit two abnormal genes, one from each parent."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000178","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What fibrinogen level is typically seen in afibrinogenemia?","answer":"Less than 0.1 g/L, with bleeding manifestations ranging from mild to severe."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000178","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What early sign often alerts to afibrinogenemia in newborns?","answer":"Umbilical cord hemorrhage frequently provides an early alert to the disorder."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000178","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What treatments are used for bleeding episodes in afibrinogenemia?","answer":"Patients may receive plasma or a cryoprecipitate blood product containing concentrated fibrinogen via transfusion."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000178","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Why is the hepatitis B vaccine recommended for people with afibrinogenemia?","answer":"Because transfusion treatment for the disorder increases the risk of hepatitis."}
